steps.body:
An `interface` names an object shape, much like a `type` alias does. For object shapes the two are interchangeable: a value that fits one fits the other, and the checks are the same.

```typescript
interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

const rex: Animal = { name: "Rex" };
console.log(rex.name); // Rex
```

`extends` builds a new interface from an existing one, so you never repeat the shared members. `interface Dog extends Animal { breed: string }` has both `name` and `breed`. An interface can extend several others at once, separated by commas, and gets every member of each.

Members take the same modifiers as in type aliases: `?` for optional, and `readonly` for properties that never change. The spelling differs only at the start: an interface has no `=` before its braces.

examples.code:
interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;
}

function greet(animal: Animal): string {
  return `Hello, ${animal.name}!`;
}

const rex: Dog = { name: "Rex", breed: "Beagle" };

console.log(greet(rex));
console.log(`${rex.name} is a ${rex.breed}`);

interface User {
  id: number;
  email: string;
}

interface Auditable {
  createdAt: string;
  createdBy: string;
}

interface Admin extends User, Auditable {
  permissions: string[];
}

const root: Admin = {
  id: 1,
  email: "root@example.com",
  createdAt: "2026-01-15",
  createdBy: "setup",
  permissions: ["users", "billing"],
};

console.log(`${root.email} (#${root.id})`);
console.log(`created ${root.createdAt} by ${root.createdBy}`);
console.log(`can manage: ${root.permissions.join(", ")}`);

interface Member {
  readonly id: number;
  name: string;
  nickname?: string;
}

function badge(member: Member): string {
  return `#${member.id} ${member.nickname ?? member.name}`;
}

const members: Member[] = [
  { id: 7, name: "Margaret Hamilton", nickname: "Maggie" },
  { id: 8, name: "Alan Turing" },
];

for (const member of members) {
  console.log(badge(member));
}

