practice.solution:
export interface Person {
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

export interface Employee extends Person {
  team: string;
  managerEmail?: string;
}

export function directoryLine(employee: Employee): string {
  const manager = employee.managerEmail ?? "none";
  return `${employee.name} <${employee.email}>, ${employee.team}, manager: ${manager}`;
}

const staff: Employee[] = [
  { name: "Ada Lovelace", email: "ada@example.com", team: "Platform", managerEmail: "grace@example.com" },
  { name: "Grace Hopper", email: "grace@example.com", team: "Platform" },
  { name: "Alan Turing", email: "alan@example.com", team: "Research", managerEmail: "grace@example.com" },
];

for (const employee of staff) {
  console.log(directoryLine(employee));
}

steps.body:
An intersection, `A & B`, is a type with every member of `A` and every member of `B`. It combines any object types, including aliases you did not write, so it is a common way to mix shared fields into a shape:

```typescript types
type Timestamped = {
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
};

type Post = { title: string; body: string } & Timestamped;
```

A `Post` needs all four properties. The same trick builds a saved record from the data a user sends: `type Task = NewTask & { id: number; done: boolean }`.

`extends` and `&` usually give the same result, but they differ when members conflict. If one side says `id: string` and the other `id: number`, the intersection's `id` must be both, which no value is, so its type becomes `never` and no object fits. You only find out when you try to create one. `extends` reports the same conflict right at the interface declaration (TS2430), which is easier to fix.

examples.code:
type Timestamped = {
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
};

type Post = { title: string; author: string } & Timestamped;

const post: Post = {
  title: "Hello, TypeScript",
  author: "Ada",
  createdAt: "2026-09-01",
  updatedAt: "2026-09-14",
};

console.log(`${post.title} by ${post.author}`);
console.log(`created ${post.createdAt}, updated ${post.updatedAt}`);

type NewTask = {
  title: string;
};

type Task = NewTask & { id: number; done: boolean };
//   ^? type Task = NewTask & { id: number; done: boolean; }

function save(input: NewTask, id: number): Task {
  return { ...input, id, done: false };
}

const first = save({ title: "Write tests" }, 1);
const second = save({ title: "Ship it" }, 2);
second.done = true;

for (const task of [first, second]) {
  console.log(`#${task.id} [${task.done ? "x" : " "}] ${task.title}`);
}

type WithStringId = { id: string };
type WithNumberId = { id: number };
type Both = WithStringId & WithNumberId;

const record: Both = { id: "1" };

interface Base {
  id: string;
}

interface Child extends Base {
  id: number;
}

console.log(record.id);

