practice.solution:
export type Auditable = {
  createdBy: string;
  updatedBy: string;
};

export type Invoice = { number: string; totalCents: number } & Auditable;

export function invoiceLine(invoice: Invoice): string {
  const total = (invoice.totalCents / 100).toFixed(2);
  return `${invoice.number}: $${total} (created by ${invoice.createdBy}, updated by ${invoice.updatedBy})`;
}

const invoices: Invoice[] = [
  { number: "INV-001", totalCents: 12000, createdBy: "ada", updatedBy: "grace" },
  { number: "INV-002", totalCents: 4550, createdBy: "alan", updatedBy: "alan" },
];

for (const invoice of invoices) {
  console.log(invoiceLine(invoice));
}

steps.body:
Declare an interface twice and TypeScript merges the declarations into one interface with every member:

```typescript
interface Box {
  width: number;
}

interface Box {
  height: number;
}

const box: Box = { width: 3, height: 4 };
console.log(box.width * box.height); // 12
```

A type alias can only be declared once, so a second `type` with the same name is an error (TS2300). Merging is how declaration files add members to types that a library or Node already declares, which this course comes back to later. In your own code, it mostly means interface names should be unique.

Some types can only be aliases: unions like `"pending" | "paid"`, tuples, function types, and types computed from others, like `(typeof ROLES)[number]`. Hence a common team convention:

- `interface` for object shapes, especially ones that other shapes extend.
- `type` for everything else.

An interface that extends another may redeclare a member with a narrower type that still fits, like a `status` that can only be `"paid"`. For plain object shapes either form works, so follow the codebase you are in.

examples.code:
// The app's core declares the basics.
interface Settings {
  theme: string;
  language: string;
}

// The editor feature adds its own settings.
interface Settings {
  fontSize: number;
  wordWrap: boolean;
}

const settings: Settings = {
  theme: "dark",
  language: "en",
  fontSize: 14,
  wordWrap: true,
};

console.log(`${settings.theme} theme, language ${settings.language}`);
console.log(`${settings.fontSize}px, word wrap ${settings.wordWrap ? "on" : "off"}`);

type Settings = {
  theme: string;
};

type Settings = {
  fontSize: number;
};

const settings: Settings = { theme: "dark", fontSize: 14 };
console.log(settings.theme);

type OrderStatus = "pending" | "paid";

interface Order {
  id: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  totalCents: number;
}

interface PaidOrder extends Order {
  status: "paid";
  paidOn: string;
}

function summary(order: Order): string {
  const total = (order.totalCents / 100).toFixed(2);
  return `${order.id}: ${order.status}, $${total}`;
}

function receipt(order: PaidOrder): string {
  return `${order.id} paid on ${order.paidOn}`;
}

const draft: Order = { id: "A-1", status: "pending", totalCents: 1200 };
const done: PaidOrder = { id: "A-2", status: "paid", totalCents: 2599, paidOn: "2026-09-30" };

console.log(summary(draft));
console.log(summary(done));
console.log(receipt(done));

